    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>An <strong>underpaid insurance claim in Dallas</strong> usually looks like this: the insurer agrees the damage is covered, sends an estimate and a check, and then your roofer or restoration contractor tells you the job will cost far more. Underpayments are not always intentional. They often come from a rushed inspection after a major storm, measurement errors, missed items, or pricing that does not match what local contractors charge. Whatever the cause, the gap can usually be traced to specific lines in the estimate. Dallas Public Adjuster helps homeowners and business owners in Dallas County find where that gap comes from and document what is missing.</p>
<p>Timing plays a part. Big North Texas storms, such as the June 12, 2023 storm that dropped hail up to 4 inches near Cedar Hill or the May 28, 2024 windstorm that hit Garland and Richardson, create a surge of claims at the same moment contractors are in high demand. Estimates written in that rush can miss damage, and prices can lag behind what local crews actually charge.</p>

<h2>Signs Your Settlement May Be Too Low</h2>
<ul>
<li>Contractor bids are well above the insurer’s replacement cost total.</li>
<li>The estimate repairs a few shingles where the roof cannot reasonably be patched with matching materials.</li>
<li>Gutters, downspouts, vents, flashing, window screens, fences, or siding are missing after a hailstorm.</li>
<li>Interior water damage is limited to one room even though stains appear in several.</li>
<li>Depreciation looks unusually high or is applied to labor.</li>
<li>Code-required items, such as drip edge or underlayment, are left out.</li>
<li>General contractor overhead and profit is excluded on a job that needs multiple trades.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where Underpayments Usually Come From</h2>
<table>
