<h2>Depreciation, Deductibles, and the Check You Received</h2>
<p>Many Dallas homeowners are surprised by the first payment because two large numbers are subtracted before it is issued. The first is the deductible. Many Texas policies have a separate wind and hail deductible, often 1% or 2% of the dwelling limit. The second is depreciation. Under a replacement cost policy, the insurer typically pays actual cash value first and releases recoverable depreciation after repairs are completed, often within a deadline stated in the policy. If your roof coverage is ACV-only or uses a payment schedule, that depreciation may never be paid.</p>
<p>Understanding which of these applies to you is the first step in knowing whether the claim is actually underpaid or simply paid in stages. Our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> can help you estimate how deductibles and depreciation affect a payment, and <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a> explains the difference in more detail.</p>

