<h2>Reopening a Settled or Closed Claim</h2>
<p>Many property owners assume that once a claim is closed, it is final. In practice, many Texas policies allow a claim to be reopened in certain situations, usually within a defined timeframe. A closed claim may deserve another look if:</p>
<ul>
<li>Additional damage surfaced after the first inspection.</li>
<li>Repairs uncovered hidden damage, such as rotted decking or wet insulation.</li>
<li>The original scope or pricing was incomplete.</li>
<li>The settlement did not reflect the actual cost of repairs.</li>
</ul>
<p>When new damage is found during work, it is usually handled as a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplemental claim</a>. Check your policy for time limits, and if you have signed any release, have it reviewed before assuming the claim cannot be revisited.</p>

<h2>Steps to Take If You Think Your Claim Was Underpaid</h2>
<ol>
<li>Request the insurer’s full estimate, photos, and any reports in writing.</li>
<li>Get a detailed, line-item bid from a qualified contractor, not just a single total.</li>
<li>Compare measurements, items, and prices line by line.</li>
<li>Document missing damage with dated photos and notes.</li>
<li>Submit a written request for reconsideration with your supporting documents.</li>
<li>If you still disagree on the amount, review the <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> provision in your policy.</li>
</ol>

<h2>How Dallas Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
